module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.SpaceRelativeProjQuotientClosedImmersion
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceMorphismProperties
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceAffineChartCalculus
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AffineProjIdealSingleDegree
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceFlatProperFpLocality
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceIdealClosedSubspaceCorrespondence
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.SpaceQuasiCompactAffineTests
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceFibreProductsEtaleCovers

/-! # Finite-degree equations for a closed subspace of relative Proj

Source: Stacks Project, spaces-divisors.tex, Tag 085M,
lemma-closed-subscheme-proj-finite
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-proj-finite-degree-equations).

The quotient is specified by the universal property for homogeneous algebra maps
killing the submodule in degree d, with arbitrary module-sheaf algebra targets.
Thus it is the quotient by the homogeneous ideal generated by that submodule.
The final isomorphism is over the original Proj and retains its closed subspace structure.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option synthInstance.maxSize 2048
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A finitely presented closed subspace of a quasi-compact relative Proj over a qcqs
algebraic space is cut out by a finite-type quasi-coherent submodule in one positive degree.
The auxiliary B and phi describe A/(F A), rather than additional input data: phi kills F,
is degreewise epic, and is initial among homogeneous algebra maps killing F. Targets in
this universal property need not be quasi-coherent. The systems C, R and Q are the existing
normalized relative-Proj and quotient systems; the conclusion holds for every such system.

Source: Stacks Project, spaces-divisors.tex, Tag 085M,
lemma-closed-subscheme-proj-finite
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-proj-finite-degree-equations).

Proof:
1. By lem-sfb-space-proj-homogeneous-ideal-recovery choose a quasi-coherent homogeneous
   ideal I defining Z. The quasi-compact atlas hypothesis and
   lem-trf-space-qc-finite-affine-atlas-refinement give an affine surjective etale atlas U.
2. By lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus, its sections give graded algebras A(U),
   I(U) and their quotient. The scheme-test comparisons and the quotient-system
   specification identify the pulled-back closed immersion with Proj(A(U)/I(U)).
   Quasi-compactness of the Proj projection gives quasi-compact Proj over U by
   lem-sfb-space-quasicompact-affine-tests. Finite presentation pulls back by
   lem-sfb-space-flat-proper-fp-locality. Apply lem-sfb-affine-proj-ideal-single-degree
   to obtain finitely many elements in a common positive degree d in I(U) defining Z_U.
3. Apply lem-sfb-space-qcoh-finite-type-union to I_d. Affine sections compute this
   filtered union by lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus, so the finitely many
   elements lie in one finite-type quasi-coherent submodule F of I_d. For an empty list
   choose zero. Compose its inclusion with I_d into A_d to obtain the mono j.
4. Form the homogeneous ideal F A and its quotient B. The generated-ideal, tensor and
   cokernel clauses of lem-sfb-space-qcoh-affine-chart-calculus show degreewise
   quasi-coherence, epimorphy, and the universal property written below: maps killing F
   kill every product with F and therefore factor uniquely through the quotient.
   The quotient system gives its canonical closed immersion into P. On U the ideals
   generated by the finite list, by F(U), and I(U) are nested. Contravariant quotient
   maps and their composition give Z_U contained in Proj(B)_U contained in Z_U,
   with the same morphisms into P_U, so the closed scheme structures coincide.
5. By lem-trf-space-fibre-products-etale-covers, P_U covers P etale surjectively.
   The defining quasi-coherent ideals of Z and Proj(B) agree there and hence globally,
   because subsheaf membership is local. Apply
   lem-sfb-space-ideal-closed-subspace-correspondence to obtain the isomorphism over P.
   Its equation with Q.map is the claimed equality of closed subspaces, including
   nilpotents. Empty spaces and the zero submodule require no nonemptiness assumption. -/
theorem exists_spaceProj_finite_degree_equations
    {S : Scheme.{u}} (X : AlgebraicSpace S)
    (hqc : ∃ (U : Over S) (a : yoneda.obj U ⟶ X.obj.obj),
      CompactSpace U.left ∧ IsEtale a ∧ IsSurjective a)
    (hqs : ∀ (U V : Over S) (a : yoneda.obj U ⟶ X.obj.obj)
      (b : yoneda.obj V ⟶ X.obj.obj),
      IsAffine U.left → IsAffine V.left → CompactSpace (Point (Limits.pullback a b)))
    (C : SpaceProjCoefficients S) (R : SpaceProjSystem C) (Q : SpaceProjQuotientSystem C R)
    (A : SpaceProjInput X)
    (hπ : MapIsQuasiCompact (R.realization A).projection.hom.hom)
    (Z : AlgebraicSpace S) (i : Z ⟶ (R.realization A).space)
    (hi : IsRepresentableWith (fun _ _ p ↦ IsClosedImmersion p.left) i.hom.hom)
    (hifp : MapIsLocallyOfFinitePresentation i)
    (hiqc : MapIsQuasiCompact i.hom.hom)
    (hiqs : MapIsQuasiCompact (Limits.pullback.diagonal i.hom.hom)) :
    ∃ (d : ℕ), 0 < d ∧ ∃ (F : X.Modules) (j : F ⟶ A.piece d),
      Mono j ∧ F.IsQuasicoherent ∧ F.IsFiniteType ∧
      ∃ (B : SpaceProjInput X) (φ : EtaleGradedModuleAlgebra.Hom A.algebra B.algebra)
        (hφ : ∀ n : ℕ, Epi (φ.degree n)),
        j ≫ φ.degree d = 0 ∧
        (∀ (D : ℕ → X.Modules) (Dalg : EtaleGradedModuleAlgebra D)
          (ψ : EtaleGradedModuleAlgebra.Hom A.algebra Dalg), j ≫ ψ.degree d = 0 →
          ∃! χ : EtaleGradedModuleAlgebra.Hom B.algebra Dalg,
            ∀ n : ℕ, φ.degree n ≫ χ.degree n = ψ.degree n) ∧
        ∃ e : (R.realization B).space ≅ Z, e.hom ≫ i = Q.map φ hφ := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
